@PACK Wild Foraging
@ENTRY safety|READ FIRST: Safety Rules
The one rule of foraging: if you are not 100% certain of an ID, do not eat it. "Pretty sure" has killed people.

Positive ID means matching EVERY field mark, not just one - many deadly plants share a single feature with an edible one. Cross-check leaf, stem, flower, habitat, and season together.

Never eat: anything with milky sap unless it is a known-safe species; white or pale-gilled mushrooms you have not expertly keyed; berries you cannot name; or umbrella-shaped white flower clusters (the carrot family, which holds our deadliest plants) unless an expert confirms it.

Even once ID'd, try a new wild food in a SMALL amount the first time - people react differently. Harvest ethically: take less than a third of a patch, never the last plant, skip roadsides (lead and spray), and know the land rules (BLM, Forest Service, state, tribal all differ).

This guide covers SE Idaho and northern Utah - mountains, river bottoms, and high-desert plains. The habitat note on each plant tells you where to look and when.
@END
@ENTRY berries|Mountain and Bottomland Berries
CHOKECHERRY - river bottoms and foothills. Dark red-to-black cherries in long drooping clusters on a shrub or small tree, ripe late summer. The FLESH makes excellent jelly and syrup COOKED, but the pits, leaves, and twigs hold cyanide compounds - never crush and eat the seeds, never eat wilted leaves, and always cook and strain.

SERVICEBERRY (Saskatoon) - foothills and open slopes. Blue-purple, blueberry-like, with a tiny five-point crown on the bottom end. Sweet and safe raw, ripe mid-summer, one of the best and easiest.

WILD RASPBERRY and THIMBLEBERRY - moist woods and disturbed edges. The classic aggregate berry that slips off its core when ripe; every red or black raspberry-form berry in our range is safe. Thimbleberry is flatter, softer, seedier.

ELDERBERRY - streamsides and moist draws. BLUE elderberry is good cooked (syrup, jelly); the raw berries, and ALL of the red elderberry plus the stems and leaves, are toxic. Blue clusters hang in flat-topped droops - cook before eating.

WILD STRAWBERRY - meadow edges and clearings. Tiny, intensely sweet, unmistakable, safe raw.

ROSE HIPS - the red-orange fruit left after wild rose blooms, best after the first frost softens and sweetens them. High in vitamin C; use the flesh for tea and jelly, and strain out the irritating seed hairs inside.

WARNING: never eat pale or waxy-WHITE berries (baneberry has a single glossy white or red berry with a dark dot - the "doll's eye" - and is poisonous), and avoid any small berries on nightshade-type plants.
@END
@ENTRY greens|Wild Greens
DANDELION - everywhere unsprayed. The whole plant is edible: young leaves before they turn bitter, flowers battered and fried, roots roasted as a coffee substitute. No dangerous look-alike.

STINGING NETTLE - moist river bottoms and shade. Wear gloves to pick; cooking or drying destroys the sting completely, leaving one of the best wild greens. Square stem, opposite toothed leaves, stinging hairs. Take the young tops in spring.

MINER'S LETTUCE - shaded moist ground in spring. A round leaf the stem seems to pass straight through the middle of. Mild and safe raw, a good trail salad.

LAMB'S QUARTERS (wild spinach) - disturbed soil, gardens, field edges. Diamond-shaped leaves with a whitish, almost powdery coating on the new growth. Cook like spinach. Very common, very safe.

PURSLANE - warm, dry, disturbed ground and sidewalks. Fat, succulent, paddle-shaped leaves on reddish stems, low and sprawling. Safe raw or cooked, lemony. LOOK-ALIKE CAUTION: spurge sprawls in the same places but has thin leaves and MILKY sap and is toxic - purslane's sap is clear and its leaves are thick and juicy.

WILD ONION and GARLIC (allium) - meadows and slopes. THE RULE: if it smells strongly of onion or garlic, it is an allium and safe. If a bulb-like plant does NOT smell of onion, DO NOT EAT IT - death camas grows in the very same meadows, resembles wild onion, and is deadly. No onion smell, no eating. Ever.
@END
@ENTRY highdesert|High Desert Plants
The dry plains between the ranges look empty but hold real food.

PRICKLY PEAR CACTUS - south-facing rocky flats. Both the pads (nopales) and the red fruit (tunas) are edible. First remove ALL the glochids - the tiny hair-fine spines are far worse than the big ones. Singe them off over a flame or scrape thoroughly, then peel. Cook the pads; peel and eat the fruit.

CURRANTS and GOOSEBERRIES (wild Ribes) - draws, canyon bottoms, rocky slopes. Golden currant is fragrant and safe; gooseberries wear small spines on the fruit. All our wild Ribes berries are edible cooked or raw, though tart.

BITTERROOT - gravelly plains in spring, a pink flower on almost no stem. A traditional staple root of the region's tribes; edible but bitter, slow-growing, and easy to over-harvest, so admire far more than you dig.

NOT FOOD, know them to avoid wasting effort: greasewood (mildly toxic), rabbitbrush, and sagebrush are not food - though sagebrush and juniper bark are superb fire tinder.

FINDING FOOD AND WATER on the plains: green ribbons of cottonwood and willow across the brown mark water and the richest foraging. When the flats look bare, walk to the green.
@END
@ENTRY nuts|Nuts, Seeds, and Trees
PINE NUTS (pinyon) - pinyon-juniper country on the desert ranges. The seeds inside pinyon pine cones are a rich traditional food; gather closed cones in fall, dry them so they open, and shake out the nuts. Roast lightly.

ACORNS - oak brush and canyon oak. Edible but bitter with tannin, which must be leached out: shell, chop, and rinse with repeated changes of water (or several soaks) until the bitterness is gone, then dry and grind to flour. Never eat them raw and bitter in quantity.

CATTAIL - marsh and pond edges, standing water. The peeled young shoots ("Cossack asparagus"), the green flower spikes (boiled like corn), the yellow pollen (a flour extender), and the starchy rhizomes are all edible - a true four-season pantry. LOOK-ALIKE WARNING: before the brown seed-spike shows, young cattail can be confused with IRIS, which is toxic. Iris leaves are flat and fan from one base; cattail leaves are rounder and taller. If unsure, wait for the brown "hot dog" spike to be certain.

MAPLE and BIRCH SAP - late winter to early spring. Tap box elder (a maple) or birch and boil the thin sap down to syrup. It takes a lot of sap for a little syrup, but the flavor is real and the skill is worth knowing.
@END
@ENTRY mushrooms|Mushrooms: Extreme Caution
Blanket rule: never eat a wild mushroom on the strength of an app or a photo. Mushrooms require expert, in-hand keying, and our region has species that kill with no antidote. This section teaches only the two safest, most distinctive, and even these deserve a mentor before you eat them.

MOREL - spring, in cottonwood river bottoms and recent burn areas. The cap is honeycomb-PITTED and FUSED to the stem, and the whole thing is HOLLOW when you slice it top to bottom. The dangerous FALSE MOREL is brain-like and wavy (not pitted), often reddish, and is NOT hollow - it is chambered or stuffed - and it can be deadly. Always slice lengthwise: hollow root to tip, or throw it out. Cook morels thoroughly; never raw, and never with alcohol the first time.

PUFFBALL - late-summer meadows. Safe ONLY if it is pure white all the way through when sliced, with no gills and no outline of a cap or stem forming inside. A young deadly Amanita also looks like a white ball at the button stage - if your sliced "puffball" shows the faint silhouette of a mushroom-to-be inside, it is a deadly Amanita, not a puffball. Uniform white inside = safe; any internal pattern = discard.

Everything else: photograph it, leave it, and learn it with a local expert. Destroying angel and some Galerina in our range cause fatal organ failure with a deceptive "recovery" phase in between.
@END
@ENTRY preserve|Preserving and Preparing
Foraged food spoils fast; knowing how to keep it doubles the value of a good day.

DRYING is the simplest: berries, mushrooms (ID'd ones), and greens dry in the sun, a low oven, or a dehydrator, then store for months in a sealed jar out of light. Dried nettle and mint make winter tea.

JELLY and SYRUP turn chokecherry, elderberry, and serviceberry into a pantry that lasts - cook, strain, add pectin and sweetener, and process in jars. This is also how you make the cyanide-bearing chokecherry pits irrelevant: you keep only the strained juice.

COLD STORAGE: roots like dandelion and the starchy cattail rhizome keep cool and damp for a while, as people once used a root cellar.

A FORAGER'S KIT: a basket or mesh bag (lets spores and seeds fall as you walk), a small knife, gloves for nettle and cactus, and a real field guide or expert on call. Photograph anything you are unsure of rather than tasting it.

Above all, forage in season (see the calendar) and take only what you will actually use - the patch you leave healthy feeds you again next year.
@END
@ENTRY seasons|Seasonal Calendar
SPRING (Apr-Jun): nettle and early greens, miner's lettuce, dandelion, lamb's quarters, cattail shoots, wild onion, and morels in the warming cottonwood bottoms. Runoff makes rivers cold and dangerous - respect high water.

SUMMER (Jul-Aug): serviceberry, wild strawberry, raspberry and thimbleberry, currants and gooseberries, purslane, and late prickly pear fruit. Carry water on the dry plains; afternoon thunderstorms build fast over the ranges.

FALL (Sep-Oct): chokecherry and blue elderberry (cook them), rose hips after the first frost, pine nuts, acorns, and late puffballs. The best travel weather, but the days shorten quickly - mind your turnaround time.

WINTER (Nov-Mar): foraging nearly stops. Rose hips may hang on, box elder and birch can be tapped near winter's end, and dried and jarred stores from fall carry you through. This is the season for the other skills - fire, shelter, and finding your way.
@END
